nce the quoting rules strip the quotes during parsing, so my `@TypeConverter` would receive the unquoted string anyway.\n\nI could also try alternative separators like `|` or `->`, but those conflict with existing syntax. Using `;` as a field separator within list elements might work better since it doesn't require quoting and would just be treated as part of the string value.\n\nLooking back at the documentation, I should quote individual list elements to avoid misinterpretation — so `[\"camera:1:DELIVERY:home\", \"lens:1:PICKUP\"]` where the quotes delimit each element and get stripped during parsing. This approach should work for the order items format, and I'll apply the same pattern to the `WarehouseInventory` table.\n\nNow I'm reconsidering whether the quoting rules for colons apply only to collection elements or to all cell values. The documentation mentions quoting when a value contains or starts with `:` to avoid map syntax, but I need to clarify whether this applies to top-level cells like `WH1.camera:IN_STOCK` or just to values inside brackets.\n\nLooking at how TableTest handles custom type converters, it seems the parser checks the parameter type first — if it's a custom type like `WarehouseInventory`, the raw cell string gets passed directly to the converter without trying to interpret special syntax like colons. So the quoting rules might only matter for built-in types that TableTest parses itself, not for custom converter inputs. I'm realizing the raw cell string gets passed directly to my converter for `WarehouseInventory`, so the commas and colons are fine. For `List<OrderItem>`, the quotes around each element signal to TableTest that it's a string, not a map, and then my `@TypeConverter` handles parsing it. I'm considering whether a simpler design would work — maybe using underscores as separators instead of colons to avoid any parsing confusion, though that feels less readable.\n\nI'm reconsidering the whole structure. I could split items into se