one location, or if they're forced to separate.\n\n## Step 4. Probe for Irrelevant Inputs\n\nPickup items don't care about delivery address, and address-based splitting only applies when everything's being delivered.\n\n## Step 5. Separate Rules from Arithmetic\n\nThe core logic is classifying which items belong in which shipment—there's not much calculation involved here.\n\n## Step 6. Frame Stateful Features\n\nThe splitOrder operation is a transformation, not something that maintains state.\n\n## Step 7. Make Thresholds Visible\n\nThe \"companion products\" concept is the key threshold—they need to ship together whenever it's feasible.\n\n## Step 8. Open Questions\n\nI'm still unclear on a few things: what happens when companion products can't be at the same warehouse, whether \"fewest shipments\" is measured per concern or across the whole order, and how the in-stock versus backordered distinction factors into the warehouse selection.\n\n## Step 9. Write the Code\n\nNow I need to figure out the types and converters for the test. I'll need to represent Order with OrderItems, where each item has a productId, quantity, fulfillmentType, and deliveryAddress, plus a WarehouseInventory to track stock levels and Shipment as the output. Since these are domain objects, I could use TypeConverters to parse them from table strings, but the real challenge is expressing complex nested objects in a table format. I'm considering different approaches for how to represent the inputs and outputs.\n\nI could use compact string representations with converters—something like `[P1:2:DELIVERY:addr1, P2:1:DELIVERY:addr1]` for order items to encode product, quantity, fulfillment type, and address. For warehouse inventory, I might use `[P1:WH1:IN_STOCK, P2:WH1:BACKORDERED, P3:WH2:IN_STOCK]` to map products to warehouses and their stock status, then represent shipments similarly as warehouse-to-products mappings.\n\nNow I'm thinking about what actually matters for testing—I want the table f